!     |                    | 1250 m2    | 1190 m2    |            |570 m2|                    |
!     |                    | 13,330 m3  | 12,790 m3  |            |6,120 m3                   |
!     |                    |            |            |            |      |                    |
!(0,77,0)__________________|____________|____________|____________|______|____________________|
!     |                                                                                       |
!     |                                Dock                                                   |
!     |                                7170 m2                                                |
!     |                                76,300 m3                                              |
!(0,108,0)____________________________________________________________________________________|
!     |       | Office 1 upper and lower                     Office 2 upper and lower |       |
!     |       | 335 m2 each                                               335 m2 each |       |
!     |       | 1,790 m3 each                                            1,79 m3 each |       |
!(0,108,0)____|                                                                       |_______|
! Floor to Ceiling Height (m) 10.7 m (35 ft)
! Floor to Floor Height (m) Office 17.5 ft (5.3 m)
! Refrigeration load is highly dependent upon infiltration rates (ACH).  Air chillers
!     and refrigeration systems oversized to allow possible increase above rates used here.
!     An effective Air Barrier System has been assumed in selecting the rates modeled here.
!     A common InfiltrationSchedule has been used for all zones with adjustments for
!     likely use made using the ZoneInfiltration:DesignFlowRate objects.
! Door openings are modeled linking Freezer1 with Subfreezer and
!                                   Freezer5 with both Cooler2 and Cooler3
!
! Internal loads associated with lighting and electrical equipment have also been modeled.
! A large number of refrigerating air coils have been modeled to allow for diversified
!     defrost schedules.  This diversity, along with the internal mass, is necessary
!     to maintain the desired temperature control.
! Internal Mass has been used to represent the warehouse contents. This correctly
!     reflects the thermal inertia of the contents, but does not mimic radiative
!     interactions between the contents and the warehouse shell. Because of the large
!     amount of internal mass, the warm-up required is longer than for some other
!     simulations.
!     Freezer 1 contents assumed to be 37 rows 7 m x 69.8 m
!     Freezer 2 contents assumed to be 19 rows 7 m x 31.7 m
!     Freezer 3 contents assumed to be 18 rows 7 m x 37.8 m
!     Freezer 4 contents assumed to be 18 rows 7 m x 31.7 m
!     Freezer 5 contents assumed to be 26 rows 7 m x 69.8 m
!     Subfreezer contents assumed to be 19 rows 4.9 m x 37.8 m
!     Cooler 1 contents assumed to be 19 rows 7 m x 69.8 m
!     Cooler 2 contents assumed to be 9 rows 7 m x 37.8 m
!     Cooler 3 contents assumed to be 9 rows 7 m x 31.7 m
!Exterior walls
!     Construction
!         Freezers   R36
!         Blast Freezer   R36
!         Coolers   R28
!         Dock   R25
!         Offices   AS0-IN45-IN45-AL11-GP02
